&lt;div class=&quot;content-intro&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About Tempo&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Based in San Diego, California, Tempo develops thermochemical energy storage (TCES) systems designed for energy-intensive industries. The platform enables industrial operators to shift energy use over time by sourcing power when it is most cost-effective and enables the electrification of high-temperature industrial heat at a cost advantage to using fossil fuels. By storing electricity thermochemically, the system delivers a continuous stream of combustion-free, high-temperature air at up to 1,200°C.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Tempo is backed by leading climate and energy investors, including Breakthrough Energy Ventures, Khosla Ventures, and Prelude Ventures.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For more information, visit &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.tempoenergy.com/&quot;&gt;www.tempoenergy.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Tempo is seeking a highly skilled and safety-conscious Electronics Technician to join our team. In this role, you will be responsible for the assembly, testing, troubleshooting, and maintenance of complex electronic systems, with a specialized focus on high power and high voltage equipment. You will work closely with our engineering team to ensure the reliability and safety of high-voltage components, power supplies, inverters, and related industrial control systems.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Responsibilities&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;System Testing &amp;amp; Calibration: Perform routine and complex testing, calibration, and validation of high power electronic systems and high voltage circuitry.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Troubleshooting &amp;amp; Repair: Diagnose malfunctions down to the component level in power electronics, switchgear, transformers, motor drives, and energy storage systems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Assembly &amp;amp; Installation: Read and interpret electrical schematics, wiring diagrams, and mechanical blueprints to assemble and install high voltage panels and subsystems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Safety Compliance: Strictly adhere to all electrical safety protocols, including Lockout/Tagout (LOTO) procedures, arc flash safety, and NFPA 70E standards.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Maintenance: Execute preventative and corrective maintenance on high voltage test fixtures and production equipment to minimize downtime.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Documentation: Maintain accurate logs of testing data, repair histories, and equipment performance metrics. Draft technical reports summarizing failure analysis.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Collaboration: Work alongside electrical and mechanical engineers to prototype new power systems, suggesting design improvements based on field testing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Qualifications&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Required:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Associate Degree in Electronics Engineering Technology, Electrical Engineering Technology, or equivalent military technical training (e.g., Navy Nuclear, Avionics).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Minimum of 3–5 years of hands-on experience in electronics manufacturing, testing, or maintenance, with at least 2 years specifically working with high power (&amp;gt;10kW) or high voltage (&amp;gt;600V) systems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Proficiency in using advanced electrical test equipment, including oscilloscopes, multimeters, megohmmeters (meggers), hi-pot testers, and power analyzers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Strong understanding of power conversion topologies (AC/DC, DC/DC, DC/AC).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ability to solder to IPC-J-STD-001 or IPC-A-610 standards.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Demonstrated knowledge of high voltage safety practices, OSHA electrical safety standards, and proper use of Personal Protective Equipment (PPE).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Excellent problem-solving abilities, strong attention to detail, and clear written and verbal communication skills.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Physical Demands&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sitting and standing —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Extended periods at a desk performing documentation work, or in the lab during hardware build and testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lifting and carrying —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Lift and carry equipment, test fixtures, or hardware assemblies up to 50 lbs.; heavier items handled with mechanical assistance or team lift&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hand dexterity —&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Frequent use of hands for assembly, fabrication, instrumentation hookup, and operation of test and lab/shop equipment&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Visual acuity —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Ability to read engineering drawings, inspect hardware, and identify dimensional and surface quality features during fabrication and testing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PPE compliance —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Ability to wear and work effectively in required personal protective equipment, including safety glasses, heat-resistant gloves, safety shoes, and other appropriate gear in high-temperature lab and shop environments&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Safety acumen —&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Demonstrated understanding of lab and shop safety protocols, including hazard recognition and risk mitigation in environments involving high-temperature equipment, energized systems, and fabrication machinery&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Travel —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Occasional travel (estimated &amp;lt;5%) for tool or material pickup&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Working at Tempo&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Tempo is a small team working on a hard problem, and the people who thrive here share a few things in common beyond their technical skills.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Clear, direct communication&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;— You say what you mean, ask when you&#39;re unsure, and keep the people around you informed without being asked. At a company our size, communication gaps are expensive — and good communicators make everyone&#39;s job easier.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Low-ego helpfulness —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;You&#39;re willing to jump in on work that isn&#39;t strictly yours, whether that&#39;s helping a teammate debug something, lending a hand in the shop, or flagging an issue you noticed outside your lane. No one here succeeds in a silo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Intellectual curiosity —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;You&#39;re genuinely interested in how things work — not just your piece of it, but the broader system. Tempo sits at the intersection of chemistry, mechanical engineering, power electronics, and software; the more you understand about the whole, the more valuable you become.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Business awareness —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;You understand that your work exists in a business context. You think about cost, timeline, and tradeoffs — not just technical elegance — and you make decisions accordingly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Comfort with ambiguity —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Processes here are still being built. You can make progress without a perfect playbook, and you know when to ask for direction versus when to use your judgment and move forward.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Accountability —&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;You own your outcomes — including the misses. When something goes sideways, the instinct here is to fix it and learn from it, not to explain why it wasn&#39;t your fault.&lt;/li&gt;
